@flexiarg math-formalization/replace-orbit-combinatorics-by-a-cyclic-module-quotient
@title Replace Orbit Combinatorics by a Cyclic-Module Quotient
@sigils [🌀/商]
@keywords cyclic, orbit, Krylov, polynomial, module, annihilator, minpoly, quotient, range, finrank, aeval
@audience lean-provers
@tone heuristic
@difficulty hard
@grade technique
@subjects linear-algebra module-theory formalization
@provenance Codex scribe positive departure: a certified finite-dimensional operator proof avoided a bounded-power independence construction by using the polynomial-module annihilator and quotient/range finrank.

! conclusion:
  When a linear-operator goal asks whether one vector's iterates span, recast
  the space as a module over the polynomial ring and compare the cyclic
  submodule with a quotient by the scalar annihilator; use kernel/range
  equivalences and finrank rather than constructing a Krylov basis directly.

  + context: You must pass between a spanning orbit of an endomorphism and a
    degree statement about its minimal polynomial, but direct manipulation of
    the first n iterates creates bounded-degree polynomial and independence
    obligations that the available API does not package.

  + IF: Polynomial evaluation supplies the scalar action, the scalar
    annihilator is identifiable with the principal ideal generated by the
    minimal polynomial, and the quotient by that ideal has a known finrank.

  + HOWEVER: Keep the polynomial-module structure and the original
    field-module structure explicit. The evaluation type synonym, restricted
    scalars, and mapped submodules are mathematically equivalent but often not
    definitionally interchangeable; transport ranges and finranks through the
    provided linear equivalences.

  + THEN: Choose or obtain an element whose `toSpanSingleton` kernel is the
    module annihilator; identify that kernel with the minimal-polynomial
    ideal; use `quotKerEquivRange` and the quotient finrank formula; map the
    resulting range back across polynomial evaluation; identify its image as
    the span of operator powers; conclude topness from equal finrank. In the
    reverse direction, use orbit spanning to show a polynomial killing the
    generator kills the whole module, then repeat the same quotient/range
    calculation.

  + BECAUSE: The quotient records all polynomial relations at once, while a
    bounded Krylov argument reconstructs the same information coefficient by
    coefficient. Kernel and range APIs expose the invariant in the form Lean's
    finite-dimensional library can transport reliably.
